\section{Conclusion}
Overlapping reserve categories create a genuine allocation problem because admitted students must be counted in exactly one category. Score constraints make feasibility depend on endogenous cutoffs, so ordinary notions of wastefulness and fairness must be read relative to the score rule, and full capacity utilization can fail to exist.

We studied two mechanisms. SPSE combines common reserve-side score elevation with deferred-acceptance logic and keeps the initial reserve capacities fixed, but it need not satisfy score-feasibility. PA-ODR enforces the score constraint by filling the open category first and converting unfulfillable reserve capacity into open capacity when necessary. Table~\ref{tab:comparison} summarizes the comparison.

\begin{table}[t]
\caption{Comparison of the two mechanisms.}
\label{tab:comparison}
\centering
\renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1.3}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\toprule
\textbf{Property} & \textbf{SPSE} & \textbf{PA-ODR} \\
\midrule
One-to-one counting & Yes & Yes \\
Feasibility & Yes & Yes, w.r.t.\ final $\hat q$ \\
Non-wastefulness & Yes & Yes, w.r.t.\ final $\hat q$ \\
Within-category fairness & Yes & De-reservation-adjusted, on final graph \\
Score-feasibility & Not guaranteed & Enforced by construction \\
Capacity reallocation & Fixed at $q$ & Endogenous, not minimal \\
Uses category preferences & Through proposals & Through lexicographic tie-breaking \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The main design tension is therefore between fixed reserve capacities and score-feasibility. Proposition~\ref{prop:spse-not-sf} shows that feasibility, non-wastefulness, and within-category fairness do not imply score-feasibility, while Proposition~\ref{prop:impossibility} shows that full open and reserve utilization can be impossible under the cutoff rule. PA-ODR restores score-feasibility by allowing capacity reallocation, but Example~\ref{ex:order} shows that this reallocation is order-dependent and need not minimize de-reservation.

